  "body": "Follow-up from #108 (branch issue/108-probe-ops).\n\nWhat happens: the descent from the rapid / safe plane to each touch's start point (and the travel between touches) is a plain G01 feed. If the part or a clamp is not where the model says, the stylus is driven into it on that move - the protected-move law only judges against the model.\n\nExpected: the positioning moves of a probe cycle are protected moves - a stop-on-contact move that alarms if the stylus trips (the G38.3 / skip-and-alarm pattern, in each dialect's own words) - so an unexpected obstacle stops the machine instead of breaking the stylus.\n\nDone means: the positioning feeds of the probe path are marked in the motion stream and each probing-capable post writes them as protected moves (alarm on unexpected contact), pinned by goldens.\n",
